HW-024  ESP32 I2S: the leak fix I published had TWO bugs of its own, and a
        DROP-only stress could not see either. This is the run that found them.

        M5Stack StickS3 (ESP32-S3-PICO-1), Zephyr 4.4.0, 2026-07-12.
        Upstream: issue #113310, PR #113311 (was: still wrong).

WHAT THE STRESS COULD NOT SEE
-----------------------------
The old stress ran 100 cycles of configure/START/io/DROP, 20 of them with TX starved.
It passed, and it was blind: I2S_TRIGGER_DROP goes straight to I2S_STATE_READY under
irq_lock. I2S_TRIGGER_STOP is the ONLY trigger that ever reaches I2S_STATE_STOPPING --
and both of the bugs below live on that path.

A test whose input never varies cannot find a defect that only appears when it does.
That is the same shape as the two earlier withdrawals on this PR.

So the stress now ends one cycle in three with a graceful STOP and a tail read, instead
of a DROP. Four builds, one board, one afternoon:

    build                                   graceful STOP     mem-slab
    ------------------------------------------------------------------------------
    stock upstream Zephyr                   drains 1 block    LEAKS: 8,7,6,5,4...
    my patch v1 (i2s_hal_tx_stop)           DRAINS NOTHING    flat 8/8
    my patch v2, part 4 reverted            drains 1 block    CORRUPT: rx=9 of 8
    my patch v2, complete                   drains 1 block    flat 8/8, canary 0/8

Only the last one is correct, and the test fails on both of my own bugs. That is what
makes it a test rather than a demonstration.


BUG 1 -- I STOPPED THE WRONG UNIT, AND IT KILLED THE BIT CLOCK
--------------------------------------------------------------
The crash the quiesce fixes is real: on the GDMA path *_stop_transfer() called dma_stop()
and nothing else, so the hardware went on writing into a block that had just been handed
back to the slab, and the audio landed on the free-list next pointer. Stopping the I2S
unit before dma_stop() fixes it.

But I did it in BOTH directions "for symmetry", and symmetry is exactly wrong here:

  RX DMA WRITES to the mem-slab block. A burst that lands after the free corrupts the
     free list. Stopping the RX unit is necessary.
  TX DMA only READS from it. A late read of a freed block cannot move one byte of slab
     metadata. Stopping the TX unit is not necessary --

  -- and it is harmful, because on this SoC the I2S TX unit is what generates the shared
  bit clock. On a full-duplex graceful STOP the TX side stops first; the bit clock dies
  with it; the RX DMA can then never finish the block it is holding; i2s_esp32_rx_callback()
  never fires again; and the stream is stranded in I2S_STATE_STOPPING forever.

Measured, cycle 3, same code, three drivers:

    stock upstream          my patch v1                 my patch v2 (correct)
    c3.stop    tx=4 rx=5    c3.stop    tx=6 rx=7        c3.stop    tx=6 rx=7
    c3.stopped tx=5 rx=5    c3.stopped tx=7 rx=7        c3.stopped tx=7 rx=7
    c3.tail    tx=5 rx=6    c3.tail    tx=7 rx=7        c3.tail    tx=7 rx=8
    drained 1 tail block    DRAINED 0 TAIL BLOCKS       drained 1 tail block

rx frozen at 7 for a full 150 ms is an RX DMA that is not running. Stock recovers; my
patch did not, every single time.

Note what the pristine non-GDMA branch actually calls, which is what I thought I was
mirroring: i2s_hal_tx_stop_LINK() -- the DMA link -- not i2s_hal_tx_stop(), the unit.
I mirrored the wrong function. (Both names do appear in the pristine driver, in
*_start_transfer(), which stops and resets the unit before arming the next transfer.
That also made a bare `grep i2s_hal_rx_stop` idempotency test say "already patched"
about a completely untouched tree; that check now matches the two-line sequence.)


BUG 2 -- PART 3 TURNED THE LEAK INTO A DOUBLE FREE ON THE STOPPING PATH
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
Giving *_stop_transfer() the job of returning whatever mem_block points at is right for a
block the DMA still owns, and wrong for one the RX callback has already handed to the
receive queue -- because mem_block goes on pointing at it. Six lines below that
k_msgq_put(), the STOPPING branch does `goto rx_disable`, which lands in
rx_stop_transfer(), which frees the block the queue is holding. i2s_read() then returns a
block that is already on the free list and frees it a second time.

The leak had been covering for this one too.

Measured with part 4 reverted -- the mechanism, step by step, in the census:

    c3.stop      tx=6 rx=7    one RX block in flight, queue empty
    c3.stopped   tx=7 rx=8    rx_stop_transfer() freed the block the QUEUE owns  (7 -> 8)
    c3.tail-done tx=7 rx=9    i2s_read() freed the same block again              (8 -> 9)
    *** STRESS: SLAB CORRUPT at cycle 4: tx=8 rx=9 of 8. ***

Nine free blocks in a slab of eight: the same block is on the free list twice.

The fix is to hand ownership over explicitly -- NULL mem_block at the instant the queue
takes the block -- after which every path out of the callback is correct by construction
rather than by accident:

    queue full     mem_block still ours        -> rx_stop_transfer() frees it   (right)
    STOPPING       mem_block NULL, queue owns  -> rx_stop_transfer() skips it   (right)
    alloc failed   k_mem_slab_alloc() NULLs it -> rx_stop_transfer() skips it   (right)
    dma failed     freed and NULLed inline     -> rx_stop_transfer() skips it   (right)

The alloc-failure row is worth spelling out, because a reviewer raised it as a second
double-free trigger and it is not one -- but only by luck. It is safe TODAY because
k_mem_slab_alloc() writes NULL through its out-parameter when a K_NO_WAIT allocation
fails (kernel/mem_slab.c). That is an implementation detail of the allocator, not a
documented contract. With the hand-off above, that path no longer depends on it.


THE CORRECTED PATCH, FOUR PARTS
-------------------------------
  1. rx_stop_transfer(): stop the I2S RX UNIT (i2s_hal_rx_stop) before dma_stop(), and
     clear dma_pending in both directions. tx_stop_transfer() is left alone.
  2. tx_callback(): NULL mem_block after freeing it.
  3. *_stop_transfer(): return the in-flight block to the slab.
  4. rx_callback(): NULL mem_block once the QUEUE owns the block.

Result: 100 cycles, 20 with TX starved on purpose, 27 ended with a graceful STOP and a
tail read, the slab flat at 8/8 free the whole way, and the canary clean -- 0 of 8 blocks
written to after being handed back.

